\b MyFps UE5.5 Technical Summary - Match Result and Ready Flow\b0\par
\par
\b 1. Goal\b0\par
This work focused on stabilizing the multiplayer match-end user experience. The target behavior was:\par
- when a player reaches the target score, every client should see the result UI;\par
- each player can click Ready for the next round;\par
- Ready state should display correctly on both listen server and remote client;\par
- when all players are ready, the next round starts after a short delay.\par
\par
\b 2. Match-End Notification Strategy\b0\par
The project originally relied mainly on replicated GameState changes and widget-side binding to display the result UI. That path was functional in some cases but fragile because client widget creation and GameState binding order could differ between the listen server player and remote clients.\par
\par
To make the result UI deterministic, the flow was restructured so that:\par
- GameMode still decides the winner on the server;\par
- once victory is confirmed, the server sends an explicit Client RPC through the custom player controller;\par
- each local client then creates or reuses its own result widget and displays the winner immediately.\par
\par
This produced the route:\par
- GameMode victory check;\par
- custom PlayerController ClientShowMatchResult RPC;\par
- local character ShowMatchResultFromServer;\par
- local match result widget ShowExplicitResult.\par
\par
\b 3. Why a Custom PlayerController Was Added\b0\par
A custom player controller was introduced because it is the cleanest place to deliver client-only UI instructions from the server. The server can reliably address every connected player controller, and each controller can forward the display request to its locally possessed character.\par
\par
This avoids depending on fragile timing between:\par
- replicated GameState arrival,\par
- local widget creation,\par
- and per-client widget binding order.\par
\par
\b 4. Ready Flow Restructure\b0\par
The Ready path was then reworked to mirror the same authoritative pattern. The final structure is:\par
- the local player clicks Ready in the result widget;\par
- the local UI immediately shows a temporary ready state so the player gets instant feedback;\par
- the request is sent to the server through MyFpsPlayerController::ServerSetReadyForNextRound;\par
- the server marks the player's MyFpsPlayerState as ready;\par
- bReadyForNextRound replicates back to clients using ReplicatedUsing;\par
- OnRep_ReadyForNextRound triggers local widget refresh.\par
\par
\b 5. Why Ready Needed Its Own Replication Path\b0\par
The key insight was that Ready is fundamentally player-specific state, not match-global state. The earlier implementation depended too heavily on GameState refresh timing to update ReadyStatusTextBlock. That caused asymmetric behavior between the listen server player and the remote client.\par
\par
The corrected design treats Ready as PlayerState-driven UI:\par
- the server owns the true ready flag;\par
- the flag replicates per player;\par
- local UI refresh reacts directly to the player-state replication path.\par
\par
\b 6. Local Immediate Feedback\b0\par
An additional local-only field was introduced in the result widget to show ready state immediately after the player clicks the button. This solves the perceptual delay that otherwise exists between:\par
- button click,\par
- server round trip,\par
- replicated PlayerState update.\par
\par
So the current user-facing sequence is:\par
- click Ready;\par
- local UI immediately shows ready;\par
- replicated ready state eventually arrives and replaces the temporary local flag with authoritative state.\par
\par
\b 7. Delayed Next-Round Start\b0\par
Another timing issue appeared when the final player clicked Ready. On a listen-server setup, the last player to click could trigger next-round restart so quickly that the local client barely had time to see the ready state update.\par
\par
To fix this, the next round no longer starts immediately when all players are ready. Instead:\par
- GameMode counts ready players;\par
- when all are ready, it starts a short timer;\par
- after the delay, it clears ready and match-result state;\par
- then it restarts the round.\par
\par
The current delay is 1.5 seconds.\par
\par
\b 8. Final Ready / Next-Round Sequence\b0\par
The resulting final flow is:\par
- client clicks Ready;\par
- local result widget immediately displays the ready state;\par
- PlayerController ServerSetReadyForNextRound RPC is sent;\par
- server sets MyFpsPlayerState::bReadyForNextRound = true;\par
- bReadyForNextRound replicates to clients using OnRep_ReadyForNextRound;\par
- local result widget refreshes ready text and button state;\par
- GameMode checks whether all players are ready;\par
- if everyone is ready, the server waits 1.5 seconds;\par
- the server clears ready state and match-result state;\par
- the server starts the next round.\par
\par
\b 9. Outcome\b0\par
With this structure in place, match-end behavior is now much more stable in multiplayer:\par
- every client gets an explicit result display event;\par
- Ready state is driven by PlayerState replication rather than incidental GameState refreshes;\par
- UI feedback is immediate for the local player;\par
- the next round does not interrupt the final ready confirmation too quickly.\par
}
